      "response": "{\"ops\":[{\"op\":\"edit\",\"id\":\"s15.s2.i1\",\"find\":\"\",\"replace\":\"We follow the principle of least privilege. For AWS and Kubernetes cluster access, we provide IAM and Kubernetes roles that grant engineers the permissions needed for routine work, and a separate, more tightly gated set of roles for destructive or sensitive actions.\"},{\"op\":\"edit\",\"id\":\"s15.s2.i2\",\"find\":\"\",\"replace\":\"<!-- vale RedHat.TermsWarnings = NO --> Prior to May 2025 we had four roles: `ReadOnly`, `User`, `PowerUser`, and `Admin`. A developer survey showed that most users defaulted to `PowerUser` because `ReadOnly` lacked access they needed, notably Amazon Athena. This gave them more permissions than they _required_. <!-- Linting rule is mistaking \\\"May\\\" as a synonym for \\\"might\\\" instead of the month --> <!-- vale RedHat.TermsWarnings = YES -->\"},{\"op\":\"edit\",\"id\":\"s15.s2.i3\",\"find\":\"\",\"replace\":\"In response, we retired the `ReadOnly`, `User`, and `PowerUser` roles in favour of a new `Developer` role that better reflected developers' needs.\"},{\"op\":\"edit\",\"id\":\"s15.s2.i4\",\"find\":\"\",\"replace\":\"However, `Developer` could update and delete Kubernetes `Deployment` resources, which is useful but could take running applications offline. We felt the lowest level of privilege should not be capable of causing a production outage.\"}],\"flagged\":[]}",
